---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = "App Store Privacy Requirements Checker - BuildPrivacyPolicy";
const description = "Free tool: select the app stores you publish to and the data your app collects, then get each store's privacy-policy requirements and the disclosure clauses you need.";
const publishDate = "2026-08-15";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<div class="sticky-header">
  <div class="header-left">
    <a href="/" class="logo-link">
      <svg class="logo-icon" width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 128 128" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="128" height="128" rx="28" fill="#1E3A5F"/><path d="M64 24C64 24 40 34 32 38V62C32 82 46 98 64 104C82 98 96 82 96 62V38C88 34 64 24 64 24Z" fill="#2E5A8F" stroke="#5B9BD5" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M52 64L60 72L78 54" stroke="white" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
      <span class="logo-text">BuildPrivacyPolicy</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</div>

<main>
<article class="page-container">
  <div class="page-card">
    <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; Tools</p>
    <h1>App Store Privacy Requirements Checker</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Published August 15, 2026</p>

    <p>Every app store has its own privacy-policy requirements, and getting them wrong can delay your launch or get your app rejected. Select the stores you publish to and the data types your app collects below to see exactly what each store demands, which extra declarations you need to file, and a merged checklist of privacy-policy clauses that cover all of them at once.</p>

    <div class="tool-container">
      <div class="tool-form" id="checker-form">
        <div class="form-section">
          <label class="section-label">Which app stores do you publish to?</label>
          <p class="section-hint">Select all that apply.</p>
          <div class="chip-group" id="store-chips">
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-store="google_play">Google Play</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-store="apple_app_store">Apple App Store</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-store="amazon_alexa">Amazon Alexa Skills</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-store="meta_facebook">Meta / Facebook App</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-store="chrome_web_store">Chrome Web Store</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-store="microsoft_store">Microsoft Store</button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="form-section">
          <label class="section-label">What data does your app collect?</label>
          <p class="section-hint">Select all that apply.</p>
          <div class="chip-group" id="data-chips">
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="device_identifiers">Device identifiers</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="location">Location</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="contacts">Contacts</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="photos_media">Photos / media</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="health">Health data</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="financial">Financial data</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="voice_recordings">Voice recordings</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="analytics_crash">Analytics / crash logs</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="ad_sdks">Third-party ad SDKs</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-dtype="account_linking">Account linking</button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <button type="button" class="check-btn" id="check-btn" disabled>Check Store Requirements</button>
      </div>

      <div class="tool-results" id="results" style="display:none;">
        <h2 class="results-heading">Store Requirements</h2>
        <div id="store-results"></div>

        <h2 class="results-heading checklist-heading">Privacy Policy Clause Checklist</h2>
        <div id="clause-checklist"></div>

        <div class="copy-section">
          <button type="button" class="copy-btn" id="copy-btn">Copy Plain-Text Summary</button>
          <span class="copy-confirm" id="copy-confirm" style="display:none;">Copied!</span>
        </div>

        <div id="plain-text-output" style="display:none;"></div>

        <button type="button" class="reset-btn" id="reset-btn">Start Over</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="how-to-use">How to Use This Tool</h2>

    <p>Start by selecting every app store where you distribute your app. Then pick the data types your app collects from users. Hit "Check Store Requirements" to get a per-store breakdown of what each marketplace demands: where to submit your privacy-policy URL, whether you need to fill out an additional data-disclosure form, and which of your selected data types trigger extra declarations. Below the store cards you will find a merged clause checklist that lists every section your privacy policy needs to satisfy all selected stores at once. Use the "Copy Plain-Text Summary" button to paste the results into a ticket or doc. When you are ready to build the actual policy, <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">generate a policy that covers these clauses</a> with our free generator.</p>

    <h2 id="why-stores-enforce-privacy-rules">Why App Stores Enforce Privacy Rules</h2>

    <p>App stores act as gatekeepers between developers and users. Google, Apple, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, and Chrome all require a publicly accessible privacy policy before they will list your app. The policy is not just a legal formality; each store reviews it during submission and can reject or remove your app if the policy is missing, outdated, or inconsistent with your actual data practices.</p>

    <p>Google Play requires every app to link a privacy-policy URL in the Play Console and complete a Data Safety form that declares exactly which data types the app collects, whether data is shared with third parties, and whether users can request deletion. Apple mandates a privacy-policy link in App Store Connect and requires developers to fill out Privacy Nutrition Labels that appear on the app's product page. Amazon Alexa Skills must include a privacy-policy URL in the Alexa Developer Console, with extra scrutiny for skills that access voice recordings or location. Meta requires a privacy-policy URL in the App Dashboard and runs periodic Data Use Checkups for apps that access Facebook user data. The Chrome Web Store asks for a privacy-policy URL in the developer dashboard and a Privacy Practices disclosure tied to the extension's permissions. Microsoft Store requires a privacy-policy URL in Partner Center for any app that collects personal data or uses network access.</p>

    <p>Getting the requirements right before submission saves rejection cycles and keeps your release timeline on track. For a deeper walkthrough of writing the policy itself, read our <a href="/resources/how-to-write-privacy-policy-for-app/">full guide to app privacy policies</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Do all app stores require a privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Google Play, Apple App Store, Amazon Alexa Skills, Meta, Chrome Web Store, and Microsoft Store all require a publicly accessible privacy-policy URL before they will publish your app or extension. Even if your app collects no personal data, most stores still require the policy link to be present.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is a Data Safety form on Google Play?</h3>
        <p>The Data Safety form is a declaration you fill out in the Google Play Console that tells users what data your app collects, whether it is shared with third parties, and whether users can request deletion. Google displays this information on your app's store listing. The form must be consistent with your privacy policy, and discrepancies can lead to enforcement action.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What are Apple Privacy Nutrition Labels?</h3>
        <p>Privacy Nutrition Labels are the data-disclosure cards that appear on your app's App Store product page. You fill them out in App Store Connect by declaring each data type your app collects, the purpose of collection, and whether the data is linked to the user's identity. Apple reviews these labels during app review and may reject apps where the labels contradict the app's actual behavior.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can I use one privacy policy for multiple app stores?</h3>
        <p>Yes. A single well-written privacy policy can satisfy every store's requirements as long as it covers all the clauses each store demands. This tool's merged clause checklist is designed to help you build exactly that: one policy document that addresses every selected store's rules in a single place.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What happens if my privacy policy is inconsistent with my store declarations?</h3>
        <p>Inconsistencies between your privacy policy and your store-level declarations (Data Safety form, Nutrition Labels, etc.) are one of the most common reasons for app rejection or removal. Stores cross-check your policy against your declarations and your app's actual behavior. Keep all three aligned to avoid review delays.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</article>
</main>

